+2.  **Training:**
+    *   The network is trained end-to-end by minimizing a loss function (e.g., L1 or L2) between the predicted signal values and ground truth data (e.g., voxel intensities or rendered images) [S1][S3, unofficial].
+    *   Typical training involves 1000 steps for image fitting, where SIREN converges to a sharp reconstruction much faster than ReLU-MLPs [S2, unofficial].
+    *   For volumetric data, architectures with 3 layers and 30-230 neurons per layer are effective for small volumes, balancing performance and memory [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Precision**: Standard grids require interpolation for off-grid queries; INR avoids this but incurs per-query inference cost [S3, unofficial].
-- **Boundaries**: Coordinates follow the $[-1, 1]$ convention [S2, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Input**: Conventional explicit voxel representations require considerable memory; INR mitigates this by representing the volume as a compact continuous function [S1].
+*   **Spectral Bias:** Standard ReLU networks struggle with high-frequency details; SIREN mitigates this via sine activations [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory vs. Inference:** While SIREN decouples memory cost from spatial resolution (fixed network size), inference cost is high as each query requires a full forward pass [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Derivatives:** SIREN supports higher-order derivatives, which is essential for methods requiring normals or curvatures [S4, unofficial][S5].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Fourier Positional Encoding](1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md)
-Fourier Positional Encoding (FPE) maps low-dimensional input coordinates into a high-dimensional space using a multi-resolution sequence of sine and cosine functions at exponentially increasing frequencies. It solves the problem of spectral bias in standard Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs), which inherently prioritize learning low-frequency signals and fail to capture high-frequency scene details. By lifting inputs to a Fourier feature space, the MLP can represent fine spatial variations without needing to learn the high-frequency basis functions itself. It differs from its parent SIREN Neural Field by using fixed (or randomly initialized) sinusoidal mappings as a preprocessing step, whereas SIREN uses sine activations within the network layers themselves.
+Fourier Positional Encoding maps low-dimensional input coordinates into a higher-dimensional space using a multi-resolution sequence of sine and cosine functions at exponentially increasing frequencies. It solves the problem of spectral bias in neural networks, where standard architectures struggle to learn high-frequency details from raw inputs. By lifting inputs to a Fourier feature space, it enables the subsequent MLP to represent fine-scale scene variations more effectively. It differs from [SIREN Neural Field](1250-siren-neural-field.md) by serving as a fixed preprocessing step for the MLP input rather than relying on sine activations within the network layers themselves, and from [Uncertainty-Aware SIREN](10449-uncertainty-aware-siren.md) by not explicitly modeling uncertainty in the encoding domain.
 
 ### [Uncertainty-Aware SIREN](10449-uncertainty-aware-siren.md)
-Uncertainty-Aware SIREN is a method for quantifying prediction confidence in implicit neural representations of steady-state vector fields, addressing the gap where preceding approaches did not quantify prediction confidence. It augments SIREN architectures with residual blocks and dropout layers to enable Deep Ensemble and Monte Carlo Dropout techniques, providing per-grid-point uncertainty estimates alongside predicted vectors. Unlike its siblings, it is the only method in its surveyed section that provides explicit uncertainty quantification, which is critical for downstream scientific analysis where decisions depend on knowing where learned representations are trustworthy.
+Uncertainty-Aware SIREN is a method for estimating prediction confidence in implicit neural representations (INRs) of steady-state vector fields. It addresses the gap where standard neural fields do not quantify prediction confidence, which is critical for downstream scientific decisions. It sets itself apart from its parent [SIREN Neural Field](1250-siren-neural-field.md) by augmenting the architecture with mechanisms to output uncertainty metrics alongside the predicted vector values, and from siblings like [Fourier Positional Encoding](1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md) by focusing on epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty quantification rather than frequency representation.
+
+### Single Field Fitting
+Single Field Fitting renders single-object fits via Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields (PDDF), requiring a single network evaluation per pixel [S5]. Normals and curvatures are obtained using one or more backward passes for the same oriented point used in the single forward pass [S5]. The implementation uses SIREN MLPs (sine nonlinearity) to allow for higher-order derivatives [S5]. In ablation experiments on the Stanford Bunny, 100K samples of each data type were used, except for the ablated one, with held-out errors measured on 25K samples per type [S5]. Ablating S-type data damages performance across all sample types, likely due to difficulties in knowing when to transition between weight field components [S5]. Ablating T-type data is the most damaging for $L_\xi$-A error, as T-type samples are effectively the hardest subset of A-type data [S5].
+
+### MDF Extraction and UDF Estimation
+This variant trains a small SIREN network $g_v$ (5 hidden layers, size 128) mapping position to $K_c$ candidate directions: $g_v: \mathbb{R}^3 \rightarrow S^2 \times K_c$ [S5]. Given a position $p$, candidates are $\{v^*_i\} = g_v(p)$ [S5]. The loss for training $g_v$ is:
+$$ L_{v^*} = \frac{1}{K_c} \sum_i [d(p, v^*_i(p)) - \xi(p, v^*_i(p))] + \frac{2\tau_{in}}{K_c^2 - K_c} \sum_i \sum_{j \neq i} v^*_i(p)^T v^*_j(p) + \frac{\tau_d}{K_c} \sum_i [v^*_i(p)^T n(p, v^*_i(p)) + 1]^2 $$
+Parameters are $K_c = 5$, $\tau_n = 5 \times 10^{-3}$, $\tau_d = 0.1$ [S5]. Optimization runs for $10^4$ iterations using Adam (LR: $10^{-4}$, $\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$) with 4096 points per update, uniformly drawn from the bounding volume [S5].
+
+### FFEINR
+FFEINR applies implicit neural representations to flow field super-resolution using spatially and temporally decoupled SIREN networks enhanced by ConvLSTM-based feature extraction to enable arbitrary-scale upscaling [S3, unofficial]. This approach has only been demonstrated on 2D flow fields [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- **Memory**: Memory cost decouples from spatial resolution; a network of fixed size can represent a volume at arbitrary query density [S3, unofficial]. The architecture size is approximately 5.7 MB, an order of magnitude smaller than CNN-based alternatives [S3, unofficial].
-- **Inference**: Inference cost requires a full network forward pass per query [S3, unofficial].
-- **Training**: Fitting the network to a large volume can take minutes to hours depending on network capacity and target fidelity [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Memory cost is decoupled from spatial resolution. A fixed-size network (e.g., ~5.7 MB for a 3-layer SIREN) can represent a volume at arbitrary query density, avoiding the GPU memory overflow associated with explicit voxel grids [S1][S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time:** Inference cost is high per query, requiring a full network forward pass for each point [S3, unofficial]. Fitting time can take minutes to hours depending on network capacity and target fidelity [S3, unofficial].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-- SIREN reaches a sharp reconstruction in ~100 steps, whereas ReLU-MLP never reaches it in 2000 steps, attributed to spectral bias [S2, unofficial].
-- In medical image compression experiments, SIREN outperforms other INR-based compression techniques using ReLU or tanh activation functions [S4, unofficial].
-- A 3-layer SIREN with 30-230 neurons per layer offers satisfactory performance for small volume data while cutting GPU memory usage [S1].
+*   SIREN reaches a sharp reconstruction in ~100 steps for image fitting, whereas ReLU-MLP fails to reach similar quality in 2000 steps [S2, unofficial].
+*   SIREN outperforms other INR-based compression techniques using ReLU or tanh activation functions [S4, unofficial].
+*   A 3-layer SIREN with 30-230 neurons per layer offers satisfactory performance for small volume data while substantially cutting GPU memory usage [S1].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-- **Strengths**: Captures high-frequency detail that standard ReLU networks smooth away; allows for higher-order derivatives (useful for normals/curvatures); resolution-independent inference [S3, unofficial][S5].
-- **Limitations**: Per-query inference cost; fitting time can be long; standard grids scale memory with resolution while INR does not, but INR requires network fitting [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** High-frequency detail representation, well-behaved derivatives (enabling normals/curvatures), resolution-independent inference, compact memory footprint [S2, S4, unofficial][S5].
+*   **Limitations:** High per-query inference cost, long fitting times, potential for diminishing returns in reconstruction quality beyond ~230 neurons per layer for small volumes [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
 **When to Use:**
-- When memory is constrained and explicit voxel grids cause overflow [S1].
-- When high-frequency details or smooth derivatives are required [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
-- For neural compression and surrogate models [S3, unofficial].
+*   Use when memory is constrained but high resolution is needed.
+*   Use when derivatives (normals, curvature) are required.
+*   Use when high-frequency details are critical and ReLU networks fail.
 
 **When Not to Use:**
-- When real-time inference with minimal latency is critical and network forward passes are too slow.
-- When the scene is simple and low-frequency, where ReLU-MLPs might suffice with less training time.
+*   Use when real-time inference per pixel is required without caching or acceleration structures.
+*   Use when the scene is very large and fitting time is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-The SIREN Neural Field was introduced as a way to encode low-resolution volumetric data as a continuous function, preventing GPU memory overflow [S1]. It was proposed to overcome the lack of capacity in ReLU-based MLPs, which have zero second derivatives and cannot model high-order signals [S4, unofficial]. The method uses periodic activation functions (sine) to enable Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) to capture high-frequency detail [S3, unofficial]. It has been applied to medical image stacks, neural compression, and flow field super-resolution [S4, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The architecture typically consists of an MLP with 3 layers and 30-230 neurons per layer for small volume data [S1].
+SIREN was introduced as a method to overcome the limitations of ReLU-based MLPs in implicit neural representations. ReLU networks have a zero second derivative and cannot model high-order signals, limiting their capacity to represent fine details [S4, unofficial]. SIREN uses the Sine function as the activation function to model fine details and enable higher-order derivatives [S4, unofficial]. It has been applied to medical image compression, volumetric super-resolution, and scientific volume analysis [S1][S3, S4, unofficial]. The architecture typically consists of an MLP with periodic activation functions, mapping 3D spatial coordinates to voxel intensity values [S1].
 
